From ca02f9b8338c79d3a49283b6d745f91b39a8ee9e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: bri-prism <288398250+bri-prism@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 5 Sep 2026 13:15:03 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] metal, vulkan: FWHT kernels for wide block widths (to prism)
 (#155)

* metal: FWHT kernels for block widths above 2048

The register-resident kernel keeps N/32 values per thread, so it stops
fitting at N=4096. Add a threadgroup-staged variant that maps one row to a
whole threadgroup: butterflies below the simdgroup width still shuffle,
those up to the threadgroup size exchange through threadgroup memory, and
the remainder stay in registers. Instantiate it at 4096 and 8192 under the
existing kernel_fwht_<type>_<n> names so the host lookup is unchanged.

* vulkan: FWHT kernels for block widths above 512

Vulkan stopped at 512 while CUDA and Metal now go to 8192. Extend the width
list to 8192 and pick the shader shape per width: the subgroup shader keeps
n/subgroup_size values per invocation so it stays on widths up to 2048, and
wider blocks use the shared-memory shader with one row per workgroup, which
keeps the register block small and the staging buffer at n floats. Rows per
workgroup becomes a specialization constant instead of a hardcoded 4, and the
value chosen at build time is recorded so the dispatch agrees with it. Widths
that would exceed maxComputeSharedMemorySize or maxComputeWorkGroupInvocations
leave a null pipeline, which already makes ggml_vk_can_use_fwht fall back.

* vulkan: key the FWHT subgroup cutoff on the register block

The subgroup shader pins BLOCK_SIZE to the subgroup width, so its register
block is n/subgroup_size rather than n/32. A width cutoff alone therefore
still admits the register wall on a device reporting a narrow subgroup: at
subgroup_size 16 a 2048 block is 128 values per invocation. Cut over on the
ratio as well as the width, which is a no-op on the 32 and 64 wide subgroups
and routes the narrow ones to the shared-memory shader.
